---
export const prerender = false;
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FaqAccordion from "../../../components/FaqAccordion.astro";

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "Is agentic AI the same as AI agents?",
    answer: "They're closely related but this page and our AI Agents Course cover different angles. Agentic AI is the underlying skill and technology: systems that plan and carry out multi-step tasks with limited supervision. \"AI agents\" is also how people search when they mean the career and job-title side of this, including AI agent jobs, salaries, and interview prep. See our AI Agents Course and Careers page for that angle.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need programming experience for an agentic AI course?",
    answer: "Not necessarily. No-code and low-code agent builders let you assemble multi-step automations through a visual interface rather than writing code. That said, agentic AI rewards technical comfort more than generative AI does, since debugging why an agent took a wrong action often benefits from understanding roughly how the pieces connect.",
  },
  {
    question: "What tools are covered in an agentic AI course?",
    answer: "A good course covers the concepts rather than locking you into one vendor: agent frameworks that chain steps together, tool use (letting an agent call other software or APIs), and no-code builders like Zapier's and Make's agent features for people who don't want to touch code. Specific platforms change fast, so understanding the underlying pattern matters more than memorizing one tool's interface.",
  },
  {
    question: "How does this connect to Lenoo's AI agent development service?",
    answer: "This course teaches you the skill of building and working with agents yourself. Our AI agent development service is us building and maintaining a production agent for your company, integrated with your systems, monitored, and supported. Some people take this course first to understand what's realistic before commissioning custom work; others go straight to having it built. See our AI Agents service page for that option.",
  },
  {
    question: "What's a realistic first agent to build while learning?",
    answer: "Start narrow. A good first project is a single-purpose agent that checks one data source, applies one clear rule, and takes one action, like flagging overdue invoices or drafting a reply to a common type of inquiry. Resist the urge to build something that handles every edge case on day one. You'll learn faster from a small agent that actually works than a complex one that doesn't.",
  },
];

const faqLdJson = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.answer },
  })),
};

const courseLdJson = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Course',
  name: "Agentic AI Course",
  description: "An agentic AI course covering how multi-step AI agents work, agent frameworks, tool use, and no-code agent builders, for professionals moving past single-prompt AI.",
  provider: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Lenoo AI', sameAs: 'https://lenooai.com' },
  url: 'https://lenooai.com/services/ai-courses/agentic-ai',
};

const breadcrumbLdJson = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://lenooai.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Services', item: 'https://lenooai.com/services' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'AI Courses', item: 'https://lenooai.com/services/ai-courses' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 4, name: 'Agentic AI Course', item: 'https://lenooai.com/services/ai-courses/agentic-ai' },
  ],
};

Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Control', 's-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=300');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Agentic AI Course: Building and Working With AI Agents | Lenoo AI"
  description="An agentic AI course guide: what agentic AI actually means, the curriculum that matters, and how it differs from generative AI and simple AI agent tools."
  canonicalPath="/services/ai-courses/agentic-ai"
  ldJson={[faqLdJson, courseLdJson, breadcrumbLdJson]}
>

<!-- ── HERO ── -->
<header class="hero-section" style="padding-top: 10rem; padding-bottom: 5rem; text-align: center;">
  <div class="container" style="position: relative; z-index: 1;">
    <div style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;background:rgba(0,227,253,0.12);border:1px solid rgba(0,227,253,0.28);border-radius:100px;padding:6px 16px;color:#a8f2ff;font-size:0.875rem;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1.5rem;" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">Home</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span><a href="/services" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">Services</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span><a href="/services/ai-courses" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">AI Courses</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span>Agentic AI Course</div>
    <h1 style="max-width: 46rem; margin: 0 auto 1.5rem;">
      Agentic AI <span class="gradient-text">Course</span>
    </h1>
    <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.72); font-size: 1.125rem; line-height: 1.75; max-width: 40rem; margin: 0 auto 0;">
      Generative AI answers what you ask it. Agentic AI does more: it plans a sequence of steps, pulls in the data it needs, and takes action with limited supervision. This page covers what an agentic AI course should teach, who's ready for it, and how it's different from both generative AI and a career in AI agents specifically. If you're searching for the job-title and careers side of "AI agents" rather than the technical skill, our <a href="/services/ai-courses/ai-agents" style="color:#a8f2ff; text-decoration:underline;">AI Agents Course and Careers page</a> is the better starting point.
    </p>
    <div class="trust-chips" style="justify-content:center; margin:1.75rem 0 2rem;">
      <span class="tchip">Builds on generative AI fundamentals</span>
      <span class="tchip">No-code and code paths covered</span>
      <span class="tchip">Hands-on agent building practice</span>
    </div>
    <div style="display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem; justify-content: center;">
      <button onclick="openModal(this)" data-source="courses_agentic_ai_hero" class="btn-primary" style="font-size: 1rem;">Request Training Quote →</button>
      <a href="#curriculum" class="btn-outline" style="font-size: 1rem;">See the Curriculum ↓</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ── PREREQUISITE NOTE ── -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="prereq-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 800px;">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
      <h2 id="prereq-heading">Start With Generative AI First</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      Agentic AI builds directly on generative AI skills. If you're not already comfortable structuring a clear prompt, iterating on a bad answer, and checking AI output for accuracy, start with our <a href="/services/ai-courses/generative-ai" style="color:#006875; font-weight:600;">Generative AI Course</a> first. Trying to learn agent-building before the fundamentals are solid usually means debugging two problems at once: whether the agent's logic is wrong, and whether your instructions to it were unclear in the first place.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      Once single-prompt interactions feel natural, agentic AI is the logical next step for anyone whose job involves repeated multi-step processes: checking a status, comparing it against a rule, and doing something based on the result. That pattern shows up constantly in operations, finance, sales, and customer support roles.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
      A useful way to think about the jump: generative AI is like hiring someone to answer a question you bring to them. Agentic AI is closer to handing someone a standing responsibility and trusting them to check on it, make judgment calls within limits you've set, and only escalate to you when something falls outside those limits. That's a bigger trust step, and the course spends real time on how to set those limits properly before you ever hand off a live business process, so the agent fails safely rather than quietly making the wrong call.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── CURRICULUM ── -->
<section id="curriculum" class="section" style="background: #e7eeff;" aria-labelledby="curriculum-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
      <p style="color: #006875; font-size: .8rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; margin-bottom: 1rem;">What You'll Learn</p>
      <h2 id="curriculum-heading">Core Curriculum</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="path-grid">
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">What Makes AI "Agentic"</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">The core distinction from generative AI: an agent breaks a goal into steps, decides what to do next based on what it finds, and can call tools or take actions instead of just producing text for a human to act on.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">Agent Frameworks and Orchestration</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">How agent frameworks chain steps together, hand off between specialized sub-tasks, and keep track of what's already been done. Covered conceptually so the ideas transfer regardless of which specific framework you end up using.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">Tool Use</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">How agents connect to other software: checking a spreadsheet, querying a database, calling an API, or sending a message. This is what turns an agent from a text generator into something that actually does work in your systems.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">No-Code Agent Builders</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">Hands-on practice with visual, drag-and-drop agent tools for people who don't want to write code. You'll build a working agent during the course, not just watch a demo of one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">Guardrails and Failure Modes</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">What happens when an agent misreads a situation and takes the wrong action, and how to build in checks, limits, and human review points so a mistake stays small instead of cascading.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">Deciding What's Worth Automating</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">Not every repetitive task should become an agent. This module covers a simple framework for judging when a process is stable and well-defined enough to hand to an agent, versus when it still needs human judgment.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">Testing Before You Trust It</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">A structured approach to testing an agent against edge cases before it touches real work: feeding it unusual inputs on purpose, checking how it behaves when data is missing or malformed, and building a habit of reviewing its decisions before scaling up how much it's trusted to do unsupervised.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7; text-align:center; max-width:640px; margin:2.5rem auto 0;">
      Each module includes a hands-on exercise, not just a demo to watch. You'll leave having actually built and tested at least one working agent, not just seen one built for you.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── WHO THIS IS FOR ── -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="who-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 760px;">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2 id="who-heading">Who This Course Is For</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      This suits people who already use generative AI comfortably and want to move from "I ask, it answers" to "this runs without me prompting every step." Operations leads, technical team members, and anyone tired of manually repeating the same multi-step check are typical candidates.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      In practice, the strongest candidates already have one specific process in mind before they even start the course, something they check or update the same way, multiple times a week, that eats more of their time than it should. If you can name that process right now, you already have a real project to point the curriculum at instead of working through generic examples.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      It's less suited to someone still building basic prompting habits, since the concepts here assume that groundwork is already in place. It's also worth knowing upfront that agentic AI has a steeper learning curve than generative AI. Expect more trial and error before your first agent works the way you intended.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      It's a reasonable fit for both individual learners and whole teams, but the two groups get different value from it. An individual usually wants the skill for their own productivity or as a resume-strengthening move. A team, especially in operations or finance, is usually looking at agentic AI because a specific manual process has become a genuine bottleneck, and someone needs to know enough to either fix it directly or brief a developer on what "fixed" should look like.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
      Set expectations honestly about timeline. Most learners can build a simple, single-purpose agent within their first few sessions of hands-on practice. Getting comfortable enough to reliably design, test, and troubleshoot agents for genuinely new situations, rather than following a template, typically takes a few weeks of regular practice on real tasks, not a single afternoon.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── SERVICE CROSS-LINK ── -->
<section class="section" style="background: #f0f3ff;" aria-labelledby="build-vs-learn-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 800px;">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2 id="build-vs-learn-heading">Learn It Yourself, or Have It Built</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      This course teaches you the skill of building and working with agents. That's the right path if you want to understand the technology, prototype your own ideas, or become the person on your team who can evaluate whether a vendor's agent claims hold up.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      If what you actually need is a production agent handling a real business process, integrated with your existing systems, monitored, and maintained, that's a development project rather than a learning one. Our <a href="/services/ai-agents" style="color:#006875; font-weight:600;">AI agent development service</a> covers that end of things, and plenty of people take this course first specifically to have an informed conversation about what to commission.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
      There's a real cost to guessing wrong on this. Teaching yourself to build a mission-critical agent through trial and error, in a live production environment, risks downtime and errors that a properly scoped development engagement is built to avoid from the start. Course-built agents are best treated as prototypes and internal tools you fully understand and can fix yourself, not as the finished system your business depends on.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── FAQ ── -->
<section class="section" style="background: #e7eeff;" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 48rem;">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
      <h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    </div>
    <FaqAccordion items={faqItems} />
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── FINAL CTA ── -->
<section class="section-sm" aria-label="Call to action">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="cta-band">
      <div style="position: relative; z-index: 1; text-align:center;">
        <p class="cta-eyebrow">Free Discovery Call</p>
        <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3vw, 2.5rem); margin-bottom: 1rem;">Want Your Team Building Agents, Not Just Prompting?</h2>
        <p style="font-size: 1.125rem; max-width: 36rem; margin: 0 auto 2rem; line-height: 1.7;">Request a quote for private agentic AI training built around your team's actual processes and tools.</p>
        <div class="cta-trust-badges">
          <span class="cta-trust-badge">✓ 100% free</span>
          <span class="cta-trust-badge">✓ No commitment</span>
          <span class="cta-trust-badge">✓ Refund guarantee</span>
        </div>
        <button onclick="openModal(this)" data-source="courses_agentic_ai" class="btn-primary" style="font-size: 1.125rem; padding: 1.125rem 2.75rem;">Request Training Quote →</button>
        <p style="font-size: .875rem; margin-top: 1rem; color:#94a3b8;">30-min call · No sales pressure</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .path-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  .path-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
